sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, docs/reference/development-containers.md#threads): Conn's own flow when a user starts a thread in an Orbit Project. It uses only public Orbit operations and T3's own API; Conn is drawn because this is Conn's flow, not a command chart. A task that attaches later drives the same thread.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · Conn thread start
  %% kind: none
  autonumber
  %% lane CONN icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane T3 icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CONN as Conn<br/>user's device
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant T3 as T3 server<br/>in the container
  CONN->>GW: List the Projects<br/>conn:project:list
  %%> What it returns:
  %%> - each Project whose default Instance runs in a container, is active and has a seed snapshot, on a Node the device has node access to
  GW-->>CONN: Projects · default's Node
  Note over CONN: Start a thread<br/>Orbit Project · message
  %%> What the user does:
  %%> - picks an Orbit Project and writes the first message
  %%> What Conn proposes:
  %%> - an Instance name from the message with a short random suffix; the Instance is always a clone of the Project's default Instance on the default's Node, with a clone of its database
  %%> - Conn keeps the message, the name and each step it finished on the device, so a reload resumes the same thread start
  %%> - the same name again resumes the device's own clone; a name that another device's create holds is refused with `instance.name_taken`
  CONN->>GW: Create the clone<br/>conn:instance:create PROJECT NAME
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the device's Node has node access to the default's Node, the same edge that Conn needs to pair
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - admits `instance.create` with the default's Node, as `instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME` does
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-create
  GW-->>CONN: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID · Instance ID
  SW->>T3: Create the Instance<br/>container/create.mmd
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the whole create: the clone of the default container and database, the workspace, the first T3 start, the Processes, the Route and the registration
  SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
  Note over GW: Record Instance, T3 server
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CONN->>GW: conn:operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CONN: status
  end
  CONN->>GW: Find the server<br/>conn:environment:list
  GW-->>CONN: environment id<br/>named by its Instance
  %%> Result:
  %%> - each server of an Instance names its `instance_id` and the container's `power_state`; Conn shows a `stopped` server as asleep
  %%> - waking it on open with container/wake.mmd comes with sleep and wake
  CONN->>GW: Pair with the server<br/>conn pairing create
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the pairing of docs/cli/conn/pairing-create.mmd: Conn follows its task with conn:operation:show and reads the link once with conn:pairing:show
  %%> - Conn pairs once: the device's own server sync joins this pairing instead of starting a second one
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-conn-pairing-create
  SW->>T3: Mint a pairing link<br/>POST api/auth/pairing-token
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a one-time link, minted with the Gateway's session; the link goes to the vault, and the Gateway reads it once
  GW-->>CONN: pairing_url<br/>read once
  CONN->>T3: Pair, then start thread<br/>first message · model
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Conn opens the pairing link, then starts the thread in the Instance's T3 project `/srv/workspace`, which the create added, with the first message
  %%> - from here Conn talks to the T3 server directly over WireGuard, as with any T3 server
  %%> - Conn sends a message during a turn with `queue`, so it never steers a turn that a task started
  T3-->>CONN: Turn streams<br/>diffs · terminal · browser
  opt a task attaches to the Instance
    SW->>T3: Drive the thread<br/>T3 MCP · thread send
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - while the task flow runs, Swamp sends turns with `queue`, waits for each by its run id and reads only that run's result, with the Gateway's session
    %%> - the user sees every turn in the same thread; a message the user sends during a turn is queued by T3
    %%> When the task pauses:
    %%> - Swamp sends nothing more, and the user drives from the thread
  end
